{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01NkG4ciYKgHCaxPXZcKeejr","type":"tool_result","content":"74\t/// ```c\n75\t/// void driver(const int *data, int len) {\n76\t///     int out[len];\n77\t///     memcpy(out, data, len * sizeof(int));\n78\t///     inner(out, len);\n79\t/// }\n80\t/// ```\n81\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n82\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(data: *const c_int, len: c_int) {\n83\t    // `len * sizeof(int)` in C: `int` is converted to `size_t`, so a negative\n84\t    // length becomes an enormous unsigned byte count (the original UB is\n85\t    // reproduced rather than \"fixed\"). `black_box` keeps the optimizer from\n86\t    // reasoning about (and deleting) that out-of-range copy.\n87\t    let nbytes = core::hint::black_box((len as usize).wrapping_mul(core::mem::size_of::<c_int>()));\n88\t\n89\t    // ---- `int out[len];` ---------------------------------------------------\n90\t    //\n91\t    // gcc lowers a variable-length array to a bare `rsp -= nbytes`: there is no\n92\t    // stack probe, no size validation and no failure path, so the VLA simply\n93\t    // *is* the `nbytes` bytes below the current stack pointer whether or not\n94\t    // that memory is usable. As a result the C library dies with `SIGSEGV` --\n95\t    // faulting on the VLA's *first* byte, which is where the `memcpy` below\n96\t    // starts writing -- as soon as `nbytes` exceeds the stack space left in the\n97\t    // running thread. That happens for oversized positive lengths (e.g.\n98\t    // `len == INT_MAX` asks for 8 GiB) and for every negative length (whose\n99\t    // wrapped byte count can never fit).\n100\t    //\n101\t    // Rust has no VLA, and the storage cannot simply be placed below the stack\n102\t    // pointer because the `printf` frames that `inner` creates would clobber it.\n103\t    // The storage is therefore taken from elsewhere, and the *fault behaviour*\n104\t    // of the C VLA is reproduced explicitly by touching the exact address the\n105\t    // VLA's first byte would occupy: where the C would have got away with it\n106\t    // this is a harmless read of stack scratch space, and where the C would have\n107\t    // faulted this faults identically -- same signal, same fault address, at the\n108\t    // same point in the call sequence (before anything has been printed).\n109\t    let anchor: c_int = 0;\n110\t    let vla_base = core::hint::black_box(\n111\t        core::hint::black_box(&anchor as *const c_int)\n112\t            .cast::<u8>()\n113\t            .wrapping_sub(nbytes),\n114\t    );\n115\t    core::hint::black_box(unsafe { core::ptr::read_volatile(vla_base) });\n116\t\n117\t    if len >= 0 {\n118\t        // Stand-in for the C variable length array `int out[len]`.\n119\t        let mut out: Vec<c_int> = vec![0; len as usize];\n120\t        unsafe {\n121\t            memcpy(out.as_mut_ptr().cast::<c_void>(), data.cast::<c_void>(), nbytes);\n122\t            inner(out.as_mut_ptr(), len);\n123\t        }\n124\t    } else {\n125\t        // Negative length: the C code declares a bogus VLA and then performs a\n126\t        // `memcpy` of ~2^64 bytes. Reproduce that same out-of-bounds copy so the\n127\t        // observable behavior stays the same as the C library.\n128\t        let mut out: [c_int; 1] = [0; 1];\n129\t        // The destination is passed through `black_box` as well, otherwise the\n130\t        // optimizer proves the copy is out of bounds and deletes the whole\n131\t        // branch instead of performing the (faulting) copy the C code performs.\n132\t        let dst = core::hint::black_box(out.as_mut_ptr());\n133\t        unsafe {\n134\t            memcpy(dst.cast::<c_void>(), data.cast::<c_void>(), nbytes);\n135\t            inner(dst, len);\n136\t        }\n137\t    }\n138\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"394bc1c5-cc6f-4095-8618-b3856a5b7c93","uuid":"52f43ea2-7702-4f3f-b1ff-7289c730ee57","timestamp":"2026-08-18T08:00:42.210Z","tool_use_res