{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01TRnU4ALm4AWt2ft96tAg2n","type":"tool_result","content":"[package]\nname = \"driver\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\nname = \"driver\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\nlibc = \"0.2\"\n\n# The C reference library (see c_src/CMakeLists.txt) is compiled with no\n# undefined-behaviour instrumentation whatsoever, and its observable behaviour on\n# a NULL / out-of-range pointer is a plain SIGSEGV. Rust's debug-only UB checks\n# turn exactly those accesses into a non-unwinding panic (SIGABRT + a message on\n# stderr), which is an ABI-visible divergence from the C for every such input.\n# The shared library must behave identically in every profile, so the checks are\n# switched off for the unoptimised profiles too. (Signed-overflow wrapping is\n# already expressed explicitly with `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add`, so\n# `overflow-checks` has no bearing on the arithmetic.)\n[profile.dev]\ndebug-assertions = false\noverflow-checks = false\n\n[profile.test]\ndebug-assertions = false\noverflow-checks = false\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\ndebug-assertions = false\noverflow-checks = false\n\n[workspace]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"394bc1c5-cc6f-4095-8618-b3856a5b7c93","uuid":"c759bdf8-852e-488b-8a28-b7c64bd97500","timestamp":"2026-08-18T09:16:09.586Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"[package]\nname = \"driver\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\nname = \"driver\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\nlibc = \"0.2\"\n\n# The C reference library (see c_src/CMakeLists.txt) is compiled with no\n# undefined-behaviour instrumentation whatsoever, and its observable behaviour on\n# a NULL / out-of-range pointer is a plain SIGSEGV. Rust's debug-only UB checks\n# turn exactly those accesses into a non-unwinding panic (SIGABRT + a message on\n# stderr), which is an ABI-visible divergence from the C for every such input.\n# The shared library must behave identically in every profile, so the checks are\n# switched off for the unoptimised profiles too. (Signed-overflow wrapping is\n# already expressed explicitly with `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add`, so\n# `overflow-checks` has no bearing on the arithmetic.)\n[profile.dev]\ndebug-assertions = false\noverflow-checks = false\n\n[profile.test]\ndebug-assertions = false\noverflow-checks = false\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\ndebug-assertions = false\noverflow-checks = false\n\n[workspace]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_sunhcnzzaeov5bexcsgfrhdxfcqs4ek5mmsqmken4zgjgkpzn4rq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. `./run_all.sh` reports **ALL CHECKS PASSED** (16/16 checks, both profiles).\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/` is one 46-line file exporting two symbols: `fma_array` (the element kernel `out[i] = mul1[i]*mul2[i] + add[i]`) and `driver` (VLA + `memcpy` + a `static inner` that runs the kernel 4-way-aliased and `printf`s each result). No features, no options, no `#ifdef`, and — verified by mechanical grep — **zero** error checks: the entire conditional surface is two `i < len` loop guards.\n\n## Phase A artifacts (all derived mechanically, all boxes ticked)\n\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — C exports exactly `driver`, `fma_array`; Rust exports both and nothing extra; `inner` stays private (it's `static`); 0 undefined non-libc symbols.\n- `ERRORS.md` — 27 rows, one per distinct rejection/UB condition.\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 25 rows over the axes the C branches on; **exactly one** valid feature combination (no `[features]`), so the suites additionally run against **both** the dev and release `.so`.\n\n## 63 differential tests, all through `libloading` (never calling Rust directly)\n\nHarness: dual `dlopen`, fd-1 capture, `fork`+`waitpid` disposition compare with a 2 s `alarm` (so \"spins forever\" is a comparable outcome), guard-page buffers, fixed-seed SplitMix64, and an independent reference model so a *shared* bug can't hide behind \"C == Rust\".\n\n## 4 real divergences found and fixed in the Rust (nothing in `c_src/`)\n\n| found by | symptom | fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| NULL-pointer rows | C `SIGSEGV`, Rust `SIGABRT` \"null pointer dereference occurred\" | the unoptimised profiles baked Rust's debug UB checks *into the shipped `.so`*; disabled in `Cargo.toml` with the reason documented |\n| `driver(data, INT_MAX)` | C `SIGSEGV`, Rust `SIGABRT` | replicated gcc's exact VLA formula (`(len*4+15)/16*16`, then `align4`) and probe its first byte, so an unbackable VLA faults at the same address |\n| `len = -512` | C `SIGSEGV`, Rust `exited(0)` | negative `len` puts the C's `memcpy` destination *above* `rsp`, in the caller's frame — the copy now happens at that same address |\n| `len = 8192` over 4096 elements | outputs diverged 3 elements past the buffer | the C VLA is on the **stack** and leaves malloc alone; the Rust's `Vec` perturbed the bytes right after `data`. Switched to anonymous `mmap` |\n\n(A 5th finding was a wrong expectation in my own test: `INT_MAX*INT_MAX + INT_MAX` wraps to `INT_MIN`, so the C prints `-2147483648`. Corrected against the C.)\n\n## Two conditions where the C has no answer to match — proven, not assumed\n\nI measured the C `.so` against **itself** rather than declaring these \"close enough\":\n\n1. **Negative `len`** — the VLA moves `%rsp` into the caller's frame. Called from four call sites differing only in caller stack usage, the same C `.so` gives both `exited(0)` and `SIGSEGV` for `len = -512`.\n2. **`len` past the caller's buffer** — 3 distinct outputs from the same C `.so` by varying only an unrelated earlier `malloc`; and `SIGSEGV` vs `exited(0)` for identical input depending only on whether a guard page or readable memory follows the buffer.\n\nBoth are documented in `ERRORS.md` with disassembly/measurement evidence, and everything the C *does* specify is still gated byte-for-byte (empty stdout for negative `len`; deterministic Rust fault; in-bounds prefix equal to the model; `err_24` backs out-of-range lengths with a guard page so they become exactly comparable).\n\n`VERIFICATION.md` is the completion record; `c_src/` sources are untouched (only `c_src/build/` was created)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":747,"cache_read_input_tokens":313159,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":747,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"394bc1c5-cc6f-4095-8618-b3856a5b7c93","uuid":"0743d82b-ef9b-4491-9eb0-fb802226761f","timestamp":"2026-08-18T09:16:29.723Z"}
